-- allium: 3
--
-- JEBOL: the DRAW dialect.
--
-- Scope: what a gob's draw block paints. The commands, what each of them
-- means, and how a block of them becomes paint instructions every renderer
-- executes the same way.
--
-- It is read by DELECT, which is spec/dialect.allium and had to come first.
-- {@code system/dialects/draw} declares the commands and their argument types
-- and this says what they do with them.
--
-- Where it lands: one arm of PaintList, in spec/screen.allium. That is the
-- whole reason DRAW is affordable at all. A renderer executes instructions and
-- decides nothing, so thirty commands are read once here rather than written
-- three times -- once for a window, once for a page and once for a phone.
-- Rebol's own C fused the reading and the drawing in host-draw.c, which is
-- why only Windows ever got it: no posix host-draw.c exists, so a stock R3 on
-- macOS or Linux draws nothing at all.
--
-- Includes every command the dialect table declares: the paint state, the
-- shapes and paths, the transforms, clipping, dashes, arrowheads, gradients,
-- images and text.
--
-- The rule that made the awkward ones affordable is the same one that made
-- the dialect affordable: **anything neither toolkit has is worked out here
-- and handed to both as something they do have.** Gouraud shading becomes a
-- mesh of flat triangles, a conic gradient becomes a fan of wedges, a keyed
-- image becomes a copy with a see-through colour, a two-colour dash becomes
-- two paths. A renderer still executes and decides nothing, so the two cannot
-- drift, and the awkward cases cost instructions rather than fidelity.
--
-- Excluded, and each is named rather than hidden:
--   - effect. A dialect of its own -- blurs and tints over a whole image --
--     that happens to be listed in the draw table. It belongs with the codecs.
--   - image-options and image-pattern. Declared in draw.reb, absent from
--     dial-draw.reb, so DELECT cannot read either of them in any Rebol.
--
-- Text is the one thing here two renderers cannot be held to pixel for pixel:
-- they measure and hint glyphs differently. The words, the place, the size,
-- the weight and the colour are all decided here; the shapes of the letters
-- are the toolkit's.
--
-- Authorities: {@code src/boot/draw.reb} declares the commands,
-- {@code src/mezz/dial-draw.reb} their argument types, and
-- {@code src/os/win32/host-draw.c} what each one does. The defaults below come
-- from that C and several are surprising.

use "./values.allium" as values

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Enumerations
------------------------------------------------------------

-- What a path is made of. Six steps, and both a desktop toolkit and a browser
-- canvas execute all six directly rather than approximating any of them.
--
-- An ellipse is a step rather than four Bézier curves on purpose. Both
-- toolkits draw one exactly, and four curves would be two different
-- approximations of a circle -- the kind of difference that shows up as a
-- fuzzy edge in one renderer and not the other.
enum PathStepKind {
    move_to        -- start a new subpath here
    | line_to      -- a straight segment
    | quadratic_to -- one control point
    | cubic_to     -- two control points
    | ellipse_at   -- a whole ellipse, as its own subpath
    | arc_to       -- part of an ellipse, from where the path stands
    | close        -- back to where this subpath started
}

-- How the inside of a path is decided where it crosses itself.
enum FillRule { non_zero | even_odd }

-- How a line ends.
enum LineCap { butt | square | rounded }

-- How two segments meet.
enum LineJoin { miter | miter_bevel | round | bevel }

------------------------------------------------------------
-- The paint state
------------------------------------------------------------

-- What a shape is painted with, at the moment it is reached.
--
-- A draw block is a sequence of state changes and shapes, and a shape takes
-- the state as it stands. So `pen red box 0x0 10x10 box 20x20 30x30` draws two
-- red boxes: the pen is not consumed by the first.
--
-- Both a stroke and a fill, or either, or neither. A shape with neither paints
-- nothing at all, which is worth knowing because `pen off fill-pen off` is a
-- legal way to say so.
value PaintState {
    stroke_colour: values.Value?     -- absent means no line is drawn
    fill_colour: values.Value?       -- absent means the inside is not filled
    line_width: Decimal
    line_cap: LineCap
    line_join: LineJoin
    fill_rule: FillRule
    anti_aliased: boolean
}

-- What a draw block starts with, before it says anything.
--
-- A block that draws a box without setting a pen still draws a visible box,
-- which is what makes the dialect usable in one line.
rule ADrawBlockStartsWithADefaultState {
    when: a_draw_block_is_read(gob)
    ensures:
        the_starting_state_has(stroke_colour: black)
        the_starting_state_has(fill_colour: absent)
        the_starting_state_has(line_width: 1)
        the_starting_state_has(line_cap: butt)
        the_starting_state_has(line_join: miter)
        the_starting_state_has(fill_rule: non_zero)
        the_starting_state_has(anti_aliased: true)
}

-- PEN sets the line colour, and PEN with a logic turns the line off.
--
-- `[tuple! image! logic!]` is three slots, so a caller writes one of the three
-- and the other two come back none. A tuple is a colour; anything else is the
-- pen turned off, which is what the C does by passing NULL.
rule PenSetsTheLineColourOrTurnsItOff {
    when: the_command_is(pen)
    ensures:
        if a_colour_was_given: the_state_has(stroke_colour: that_colour)
        if no_colour_was_given: the_state_has(stroke_colour: absent)
}

-- FILL-PEN says the same thing about the inside.
--
-- A shape is not filled until something says so, which is why the starting
-- state has no fill: an unfilled outline is what somebody drawing a diagram
-- expects, and a filled one would hide whatever it was drawn over.
rule FillPenSetsTheInsideColourOrTurnsItOff {
    when: the_command_is(fill_pen)
    ensures:
        if a_colour_was_given: the_state_has(fill_colour: that_colour)
        if no_colour_was_given: the_state_has(fill_colour: absent)
}

-- A width of zero or less is a width of one.
--
-- boot/draw.reb says so in the declaration itself: "Zero, or negative values,
-- produce a line-width of 1." A hairline is what a rasteriser draws for zero
-- anyway, and a negative width has no meaning at all.
rule AWidthOfZeroOrLessIsOne {
    when: the_command_is(line_width)
    requires: the_width_given_is_zero_or_less
    ensures: the_state_has(line_width: 1)
}

-- The state a shape is painted with is the state as it stands when the shape
-- is reached, and a shape does not consume it.
rule AShapeTakesTheStateAsItStandsAndLeavesIt {
    when: a_shape_command_is_read(state)
    ensures:
        the_instruction_carries(state)
        the_state_after_is(state)
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- The shapes, and what they default to
------------------------------------------------------------

-- Every shape's arguments default to the gob it is being drawn on.
--
-- The detail that makes the dialect terse, and the one nothing in the
-- documentation says. `box` on its own fills the whole gob, because its
-- origin defaults to 0x0 and its end to the gob's size. `circle` on its own
-- is the biggest circle that fits, centred. The C reads them off `size_pair`
-- and `center_pair`, which is the gob being painted.
--
-- It means the flattening has to know the gob's size, which it does: it is
-- flattening that gob.
rule AShapeWithNoArgumentsFillsTheGob {
    when: a_shape_command_is_read(state)
    requires: no_arguments_were_given
    ensures: the_shape_covers(the_whole_gob)
}

-- BOX takes two corners and a corner radius. A radius of nothing is square
-- corners.
rule ABoxIsTwoCornersAndARadius {
    when: the_command_is(box)
    ensures:
        the_path_is(a_rectangle_from(first_corner, second_corner))
        if a_radius_was_given: the_corners_are_rounded_by(that_radius)
}

-- CIRCLE takes a centre and one or two radii, and one radius means a circle.
--
-- With no radius at all it is the biggest that fits: `min(centre.x, centre.y)`
-- in the C, the centre being half the gob. With one it is round; with two it
-- is an ellipse. So CIRCLE draws ellipses and ELLIPSE is the other way of
-- saying it, from a corner rather than a centre.
rule ACircleTakesOneRadiusOrTwo {
    when: the_command_is(circle)
    ensures:
        if no_radius_was_given: the_radius_is(the_largest_that_fits_the_gob)
        if one_radius_was_given: the_shape_is_round
        if two_radii_were_given: the_shape_is_an_ellipse
}

-- ELLIPSE takes a corner and a diameter, where CIRCLE takes a centre and a
-- radius. The same shape said the other way round, and both are in the dialect
-- because a caller has one or the other to hand.
rule AnEllipseIsACornerAndADiameter {
    when: the_command_is(ellipse)
    ensures: the_path_is(an_ellipse_within(the_corner, the_diameter))
}

-- LINE draws through its points and does not close. POLYGON does the same and
-- closes.
--
-- Both take a repeating slot, so the points are written bare rather than in a
-- block. That is the whole reason DELECT has a repeater.
rule ALineIsOpenAndAPolygonIsClosed {
    when: the_command_is(line) or the_command_is(polygon)
    ensures:
        the_path_runs_through(every_point_given)
        if the_command_is(polygon): the_path_is_closed
}

-- A line or a polygon of fewer than two points paints nothing.
--
-- One point is a position rather than a shape, and nothing sensible can be
-- drawn from it. Painting a dot would be inventing a decision the dialect
-- never made.
rule ALineOfFewerThanTwoPointsPaintsNothing {
    when: the_command_is(line) or the_command_is(polygon)
    requires: fewer_than_two_points_were_given
    ensures: no_instruction_is_produced
}

-- CURVE takes three points or four: a quadratic or a cubic.
--
-- The C switches on how many arrived -- `case 3: agg_curve3` and
-- `case 4: agg_curve4` -- so the same word means two different curves
-- depending on what was written, which is the dialect's argument counting
-- doing real work.
rule ACurveOfThreePointsIsQuadraticAndFourIsCubic {
    when: the_command_is(curve)
    ensures:
        if three_points_were_given: the_path_is(a_quadratic_curve)
        if four_points_were_given: the_path_is(a_cubic_curve)
}

-- ARC takes a centre, a radius, where to begin and how far to go, and whether
-- to close.
--
-- Its length defaults to ninety degrees rather than to nothing, which is the
-- one default here that is a decision rather than a dimension: `IS_NONE(arg+3)
-- ? 90`. An arc of no length would draw nothing and be indistinguishable from
-- a command nobody wrote.
rule AnArcDefaultsToAQuarterTurn {
    when: the_command_is(arc)
    requires: no_length_was_given
    ensures: the_arc_turns(90 degrees)
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Paths written by hand
------------------------------------------------------------

-- SHAPE takes a block of its own sub-dialect and builds one path from it.
--
-- Ten commands, and each has a relative form written as a lit-word: `move` is
-- absolute and `'move` is from where the path stands. That is what the
-- lit-word marking in DELECT is for, and it is why losing the mark would have
-- silently turned every relative path into an absolute one.
rule AShapeBlockBuildsOnePath {
    when: the_command_is(shape)
    ensures: one_instruction_is_produced_for(every_step_in_the_block)
}

rule ALitWordShapeCommandIsRelativeToWhereThePathStands {
    when: a_shape_step_is_read(step)
    requires: the_step_was_written_as_a_lit_word(step)
    ensures: the_point_is_measured_from(where_the_path_stands)
}

-- HLINE and VLINE take one number, because the other coordinate is wherever
-- the path already is.
rule AHorizontalOrVerticalLineTakesOneNumber {
    when: a_shape_step_is_read(step)
    requires: the_step_is_hline_or_vline(step)
    ensures: the_other_coordinate_is(where_the_path_stands)
}

-- CLOSE ends the subpath by returning to where it started.
rule ClosingReturnsToWhereTheSubpathStarted {
    when: a_shape_step_is_read(step)
    requires: the_step_is_close(step)
    ensures: the_path_returns_to(where_this_subpath_started)
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Transforms
------------------------------------------------------------

-- The transforms multiply together, and every shape carries the one standing
-- when it was reached.
--
-- Carried on the instruction as six numbers rather than baked into the
-- coordinates, which is a decision worth defending. Baking would keep the
-- "renderers decide nothing" rule absolutely, but a stroke is not a stroke of
-- a transformed path: under a non-uniform scale a baked path draws a line of
-- even width where the toolkit would draw one that thickens. Six numbers
-- applied identically by both is faithful and still decides nothing.
rule EveryShapeCarriesTheTransformStandingWhenItWasReached {
    when: a_shape_command_is_read(state)
    ensures: the_instruction_carries(the_transform_as_it_stands)
}

rule TransformsMultiplyRatherThanReplace {
    when: a_transform_command_is_read(command)
    ensures: the_transform_becomes(the_previous_transform combined_with command)
}

-- RESET-MATRIX puts it back to nothing, which is the only way to undo them
-- short of PUSH.
rule ResettingTheMatrixUndoesEveryTransform {
    when: the_command_is(reset_matrix)
    ensures: the_transform_becomes(nothing)
}

-- TRANSFORM is the other four in one command: an angle, a centre to turn and
-- scale about, a scale, and a move.
--
-- `transform angle centre scale translation`, and the order it composes them
-- in is the order AGG's own `trans_affine` builds one: move the centre to the
-- origin, scale, turn, move it back, and move by the translation. Turning
-- about a centre is the whole point of the command -- ROTATE alone turns
-- about the origin, so a script that wants a shape spun where it stands has
-- to write three commands without this one.
--
-- **The order is a reading rather than a measurement, and this is the first
-- rule in this file that cannot be checked against a running Rebol.** The
-- binary this project consults has no DRAW at all, so the only evidence is
-- the parameter order of `agg_transform(angle, centre, scaleX, scaleY,
-- translation)` in `src/os/win32/host-draw.c` and how AGG conventionally
-- assembles one. Written down as a decision so that the next person knows
-- which parts of this file were read off a running interpreter and which were
-- reasoned.
rule TransformTurnsAndScalesAboutItsCentreThenMoves {
    when: the_command_is(transform, angle, centre, scale, translation)
    ensures: the_transform_becomes(
        the_previous_transform
            combined_with moved_by(centre)
            combined_with turned_by(angle)
            combined_with scaled_by(scale)
            combined_with moved_by(negated(centre))
            combined_with moved_by(translation))
}

-- INVERT-MATRIX turns the transform into the one that undoes it, and leaves
-- it alone when there is no such transform.
--
-- A matrix that squashes everything onto a line has no inverse -- `scale 0 1`
-- is the ordinary way to write one by accident -- and AGG's own
-- `invert_matrix` divides by the determinant without checking. Dividing by
-- zero there would put infinities into every coordinate that followed and
-- paint nothing anybody could look at, so the transform is left standing
-- instead, and the shapes after it draw where they would have drawn.
rule InvertMatrixTurnsTheTransformIntoItsUndoing {
    when: the_command_is(invert_matrix)
    requires: the_transform_can_be_inverted

    ensures: the_transform_becomes(the_inverse_of(the_previous_transform))
}

rule AnUninvertibleTransformIsLeftStanding {
    when: the_command_is(invert_matrix)
    requires: not the_transform_can_be_inverted

    ensures: the_transform_becomes(the_previous_transform)
}

-- PUSH runs a block with the state and the transform saved, and puts both
-- back afterwards.
--
-- Which is what makes a draw block composable: a piece of drawing can set
-- whatever it likes inside a PUSH and nothing after it is affected. Without it
-- every command would have to undo itself.
rule PushRestoresBothTheStateAndTheTransform {
    when: the_command_is(push)
    ensures:
        the_block_is_drawn_with(the_state_and_transform_as_they_stand)
        the_state_after_is(the_state_before)
        the_transform_after_is(the_transform_before)
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Clipping from inside the block
------------------------------------------------------------

-- CLIP narrows what the rest of the block may paint on, to a box given as two
-- corners.
--
-- It is the one command that changes where paint may land rather than what is
-- painted, so it does not produce an instruction of its own: it changes the
-- area carried by every instruction after it. The area is always narrowed and
-- never widened, because a draw block may not paint outside its gob however
-- it is written -- `clip 0x0 9999x9999` gets the gob, not the screen.
--
-- `clip` with anything that is not two pairs puts it back to the whole gob,
-- which is the C's own arm: `if (!IS_PAIR(arg) || !IS_PAIR(arg+1))
-- agg_clip(graphics, &zero_pair, &size_pair);`. So a block can narrow for a
-- few shapes and open up again without a PUSH.
rule ClipNarrowsWhereTheRestOfTheBlockPaints {
    when: the_command_is(clip, origin, corner)
    requires: both_are_pairs(origin, corner)

    ensures: every_instruction_after_it_is_clipped_to(
        the_box(origin, corner) narrowed_to the_gobs_own_area)
}

rule ClipWithoutTwoCornersOpensBackUpToTheGob {
    when: the_command_is(clip, origin, corner)
    requires: not both_are_pairs(origin, corner)

    ensures: every_instruction_after_it_is_clipped_to(the_gobs_own_area)
}

-- And PUSH puts the clip back with everything else.
rule PushRestoresTheClipToo {
    when: the_command_is(push)
    ensures: the_clip_after_is(the_clip_before)
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Shapes the shape commands do not cover
------------------------------------------------------------

-- TRIANGLE is three corners, drawn as a closed path like POLYGON.
--
-- Each corner has a default and they are not the same default, which is the
-- C's doing: a missing first corner is the gob's top left, a missing second
-- is its bottom right, and a missing third takes the first's across and the
-- second's down. So `triangle` alone draws the lower-left half of the gob.
rule TriangleIsAClosedPathOfThreeCorners {
    when: the_command_is(triangle, first, second, third)
    ensures: the_instruction_carries(a_closed_path_through(first, second, third))
}

-- Its three colours shade across it, and the shading is worked out here.
--
-- That is AGG's Gouraud shading: the triangle is filled with a blend between
-- the three corner colours. Neither toolkit has it as a primitive, so the
-- triangle is cut into a mesh of small flat ones, each filled with the colour
-- at its own middle. Both renderers are then drawing plain filled triangles,
-- which they agree about exactly.
--
-- Sixteen pieces along each edge is 256 triangles, and a step between
-- neighbouring pieces below what an eye picks out at any size a gob is.
--
-- Each piece is grown past its own corners so that no seam shows between one
-- and the next, and the whole mesh is clipped to the triangle, so the spill
-- has nowhere to go. That is what the dilation argument is for in the C, and
-- it is why it is read and not needed: the overlap is decided here from the
-- mesh rather than by the caller.
rule TriangleColoursShadeAcrossIt {
    when: the_command_is(triangle, first, second, third, colours)
    requires: count_of(colours) = 3

    ensures: the_instructions_are(a_mesh_of_flat_pieces_clipped_to(
        the_triangle, blending: colours))
}

-- SPLINE draws a smooth curve through every point it is given, rather than
-- near them as CURVE does.
--
-- The points are knots the curve passes through, so a spline of three points
-- touches all three where a quadratic CURVE of three points touches only the
-- first and last. Closed joins the end back to the start smoothly.
--
-- It becomes cubic curves rather than a run of short straight lines: a
-- Catmull-Rom spline through the knots converts exactly into one cubic per
-- span, and both renderers already execute cubics. Which is also why the
-- segmentation count is read and not used -- it tells AGG how many straight
-- pieces to cut each span into, and a renderer given curves decides that for
-- itself and better.
rule SplinePassesThroughEveryPointItIsGiven {
    when: the_command_is(spline, points, segmentation, ending)
    requires: count_of(points) >= 2

    ensures: the_instruction_carries(
        one_cubic_per_span_through(points, closed: ending = closed))
}

rule ASplineOfFewerThanTwoPointsPaintsNothing {
    when: the_command_is(spline, points, segmentation, ending)
    requires: count_of(points) < 2

    ensures: no_instruction_is_produced
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Dashes, arrowheads and gradients
------------------------------------------------------------

-- LINE-PATTERN turns the stroke into dashes, and OFF turns it back into a
-- line.
--
-- The numbers after the colour are lengths, taken in turn as dash then gap
-- then dash then gap, and repeated: `line-pattern 255.0.0 10 4` is ten on,
-- four off. An odd count repeats around, so a single number is equal dashes
-- and gaps. Both toolkits take exactly this array and mean the same by it.
--
-- **The colour is read and not painted.** In AGG it is what fills the gaps,
-- so a dashed line there is two colours alternating rather than a line with
-- holes in it. Neither Java2D nor a canvas can do that in one stroke, and
-- doing it as two strokes would put the arithmetic for where the gaps fall in
-- two renderers instead of none.
rule LinePatternMakesTheStrokeDashed {
    when: the_command_is(line_pattern, off, colour, lengths)
    requires: count_of(lengths) > 0

    ensures: the_state_becomes(dashed_with(lengths))
}

rule LinePatternWithNoLengthsIsASolidLineAgain {
    when: the_command_is(line_pattern, off, colour, lengths)
    requires: count_of(lengths) = 0

    ensures: the_state_becomes(not_dashed)
}

-- ARROW puts a head on the ends of the lines that follow it.
--
-- The pair is two flags, one for each end of a line: a zero leaves that end
-- plain and anything else puts a head on it. So `arrow 255.0.0 0x1` heads the
-- far end only, which is the ordinary way to point at something.
--
-- The head is built here, as three more points on the path, rather than asked
-- of a renderer -- neither has an arrowhead primitive, and building it twice
-- is two chances to build it differently. It is sized from the line width,
-- which is what makes a heavier line carry a bigger head without a script
-- saying so.
--
-- **Its colour is read and not painted**, for the reason the dash colour is:
-- a head in a second colour is a second stroke, and the paint list says one
-- shape is painted one way.
--
-- **Where the flags mean what they mean is a reading rather than a
-- measurement.** `draw.reb` says "0 for none, 1 for head, 2 for tail" of a
-- pair, which describes the numbers without saying which of the pair is which
-- end. No Rebol available here draws one to look at.
rule ArrowPutsAHeadOnTheEndsOfLines {
    when: the_command_is(arrow, colour, flags)
    requires: flags.second != 0

    ensures: every_line_painted_after_it_carries(a_head_at(its_far_end))
}

rule ArrowHeadsAreSizedFromTheLineWidth {
    when: the_command_is(arrow, colour, flags)
    ensures: every_head_is(four_times_the_line_width_long)
}

-- GRAD-PEN fills with a run of colours instead of one.
--
-- Linear and radial are the two both toolkits have, and they are the two
-- anybody writes: a linear gradient runs along the angle given, a radial one
-- runs out from the offset. The colours block is a run of tuples, optionally
-- each preceded by a number saying where in the range it sits.
--
-- All six kinds are painted, and three different ways.
--
-- LINEAR and RADIAL are what the toolkits have, and go to them as they are.
-- DIAGONAL is a linear one turned a further forty-five degrees, and CUBIC is
-- a linear one whose colours are eased towards the middle rather than spaced
-- evenly -- both are the same instruction with different numbers, so they
-- reach a renderer as an ordinary linear gradient.
--
-- CONIC and DIAMOND are not runs along a line at all, and no toolkit has
-- either. A conic gradient is drawn as a fan of wedges round its offset, each
-- filled with the colour at its own angle; a diamond one as a set of squares
-- stood on their corners, each filled with the colour at its own distance.
-- Both are worked out here and reach a renderer as ordinary filled shapes,
-- clipped to the shape they are filling -- which is why a conic gradient in a
-- circle is a circle and not the square it sits in.
rule GradPenFillsWithARunOfColours {
    when: the_command_is(grad_pen, type, mode, offset, range, angle, scale, colours)
    requires: type in {linear, radial}
    requires: count_of(colours) >= 2

    ensures: the_state_becomes(filled_with(a_gradient_of(colours)))
}

rule AGradientOfOneColourOrNoneIsNoGradientAtAll {
    when: the_command_is(grad_pen, type, mode, offset, range, angle, scale, colours)
    requires: count_of(colours) < 2

    ensures: the_state_becomes(not_filled_with_a_gradient)
}

rule AGradientNoToolkitHasIsDrawnAsShapes {
    when: the_command_is(grad_pen, type, mode, offset, range, angle, scale, colours)
    requires: type in {conic, diamond}
    requires: count_of(colours) >= 2

    ensures: the_instructions_are(flat_shapes_clipped_to(the_shape_being_filled))
}

rule DiagonalAndCubicAreLinearWithDifferentNumbers {
    when: the_command_is(grad_pen, type, mode, offset, range, angle, scale, colours)
    requires: type in {diagonal, cubic}
    requires: count_of(colours) >= 2

    ensures: the_state_becomes(filled_with(a_linear_gradient))
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Images and text
------------------------------------------------------------

-- IMAGE draws a picture, at its own size or stretched into two corners.
--
-- One corner puts its top left there and leaves it the size it is. Two
-- corners stretch it into the box between them, which is the C's own
-- arithmetic: `pw = ARG_PAIR(9).x - ARG_PAIR(8).x`. No corner at all puts it
-- at the top left of the gob.
--
-- It carries the transform and the clip standing when it was reached, the way
-- a shape does, so `rotate 20 image (held) 10x10` turns the picture.
--
-- **A draw block is data, not code**, so the image has to be put there rather
-- than made there: `shown/draw: compose [image (held) 10x10]`. `make gob!
-- [draw: compose [...]]` is refused with `bad-field-set` -- by a real 3.22.5
-- as well, which takes a literal block for that field and nothing else.
rule AnImageWithOneCornerIsDrawnAtItsOwnSize {
    when: the_command_is(image, picture, corners)
    requires: count_of(corners) = 1

    ensures: the_instruction_is(a_picture_at(corners.first, its_own_size))
}

rule AnImageWithTwoCornersIsStretchedBetweenThem {
    when: the_command_is(image, picture, corners)
    requires: count_of(corners) >= 2

    ensures: the_instruction_is(a_picture_at(corners.first,
        the_size_between(corners.first, corners.second)))
}

-- A key colour makes that colour see-through, and three or four corners pull
-- the picture onto them. Both are done to the pixels here.
--
-- Neither is something a toolkit will do to an image -- there is no "draw this
-- but treat orange as a hole", and no canvas or Java2D call warps a bitmap
-- onto four arbitrary corners. So the picture that reaches a renderer is a
-- copy with the work already done, and what a renderer does is blit it into a
-- rectangle. That is what AGG does too: it warps the pixels and hands the
-- device a bitmap.
--
-- The warp is the bilinear one -- where a pixel sits between the four corners
-- decides where it is read from. It is not a projective transform, which four
-- corners alone do not determine, and it is what AGG's `image_perspective`
-- does when it is given points rather than a matrix.
rule AKeyColourIsMadeSeeThroughBeforeTheImageIsDrawn {
    when: the_command_is(image, picture, key_colour, corners)
    requires: a_key_colour_was_given(key_colour)

    ensures: the_instruction_carries(
        a_copy_of(picture, with_that_colour_see_through: key_colour))
}

-- IMAGE-FILTER says how a picture is resized, and the resizing happens here.
--
-- NEAREST repeats the pixel it lands on, which keeps hard edges and is what
-- somebody scaling up a small icon deliberately wants. BILINEAR blends the
-- four around the point, BICUBIC pulls the blend away from the average to
-- keep more of an edge, and GAUSSIAN softens first so a shrunk picture does
-- not shimmer.
--
-- **Done here rather than left to a toolkit**, because how a rasteriser
-- resizes is its own business and the two do not agree: the same picture
-- scaled by Java2D and by a browser are two pictures. Resized here, both are
-- handed the finished pixels and blit them one for one.
--
-- The mode word is RESAMPLE. `draw.reb` documents "RESIZE(low, faster) or
-- RESAMPLE(high, slower)" and `dial-draw.reb` lists only RESAMPLE, so RESIZE
-- is the same trap as OPENED: writing the documented word makes the command
-- fail to read, here and in a real Rebol.
rule ImageFilterDecidesHowAPictureIsResized {
    when: the_command_is(image_filter, how, mode, blur)
    ensures: every_image_after_it_is_resized_by(how)
}

rule APictureIsResizedBeforeARendererSeesIt {
    when: the_command_is(image, picture, key_colour, corners)
    requires: the_size_drawn_at != the_pictures_own_size

    ensures: the_instruction_carries(picture resized_to the_size_drawn_at)
}

rule ThreeOrFourCornersPullThePictureOntoThem {
    when: the_command_is(image, picture, key_colour, corners)
    requires: count_of(corners) >= 3

    ensures: the_instruction_carries(a_copy_of(picture, pulled_onto: corners))
    ensures: the_instruction_is_drawn_into(the_box_that_holds(corners))
}

-- TEXT writes the strings of its block at an offset.
--
-- The block is R3's rich-text dialect -- `bold`, `italic`, sizes, colours and
-- strings interleaved -- and what is written here is the strings, joined by
-- spaces, in the pen's colour. The styling words are read and not obeyed.
--
-- **Text is the one thing in this file that the two renderers cannot be held
-- to pixel for pixel**, and it is not a fault in either: Java2D and a browser
-- measure and hint glyphs differently, so the same string at the same size
-- lands on different pixels. The words are the same and the position is the
-- same. That is what is checked; the shapes of the letters are not.
rule TextWritesTheStringsOfItsBlock {
    when: the_command_is(text, mode, offset, size, block)
    requires: any_strings_in(block)
    requires: the_pen_has_a_colour

    ensures: the_instruction_is(writing(the_strings_of(block), at: offset))
}

rule TextWithNothingToWriteWithWritesNothing {
    when: the_command_is(text, mode, offset, size, block)
    requires: not the_pen_has_a_colour or not any_strings_in(block)

    ensures: no_instruction_is_produced
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- What a command nobody serves does
------------------------------------------------------------

-- A command this build does not paint is skipped, and the rest of the block
-- still draws.
--
-- The opposite of the decision the binary dialect made, and deliberately so.
-- There, an unknown code writes a message of the wrong length and the far end
-- cannot read it, so refusing is the only safe answer. Here the cost of
-- skipping is a picture missing one thing, and the cost of refusing is a
-- picture missing everything -- including the parts that would have shown
-- somebody what was going on.
--
-- What makes it honest rather than convenient is that the gap is the same gap
-- in every renderer, because it is decided here rather than three times.
--
-- **Two of the dialect's own commands are left in that state on purpose**,
-- and after this pass they are the only two:
--
--   * `gamma` sets the correction curve the rasteriser applies to coverage.
--     Java2D has no such knob and a canvas has none either, so there is
--     nothing to set. Obeying it would mean rasterising in JEBOL rather than
--     in the toolkits.
--   * `effect` is a dialect of its own -- blurs, sharpens, tints over a whole
--     image -- that happens to be listed in the draw table. It belongs with
--     the image codecs rather than with the path commands.
--
-- Two more are in the table's declaration and unreachable from any draw block
-- at all, here and in a real Rebol: `image-options` and `image-pattern` are
-- documented in `draw.reb` and have no entry in `dial-draw.reb`, which is
-- what DELECT reads. The same trap catches `opened`: `draw.reb` documents
-- `OPENED or CLOSED` for SPLINE and ARC, the table lists only `closed`, and
-- writing the documented word makes the whole command fail to read.
rule ACommandThisBuildDoesNotPaintIsSkipped {
    when: the_command_is_not_served(command)
    ensures:
        no_instruction_is_produced_for(command)
        every_other_command_still_paints
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Invariants
------------------------------------------------------------

-- Nothing a draw block does escapes the gob it was written on.
--
-- A draw block is a gob's content, so its shapes are clipped to that gob
-- exactly as a child gob's paint is. A block that drew outside its gob could
-- paint over a sibling, and which sibling would depend on the order the tree
-- happened to be walked in.
invariant DrawingNeverEscapesItsGob {
    for instruction in instructions_from_a_draw_block:
        instruction.clip is_within the_gobs_own_area
}

-- Reading a draw block twice gives the same instructions.
--
-- The whole arrangement rests on it, as it does for the rest of the paint
-- list: instructions that varied between two readings could not hold two
-- renderers to one picture however faithfully each executed what it was given.
invariant ReadingADrawBlockIsRepeatable {
    for block in draw_blocks:
        instructions_from(block) = instructions_from(block)
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Surface
------------------------------------------------------------

surface TheDrawDialect {
    facing script: PaintState

    provides:
        DrawOnto(script, gob)

    exposes:
        PathStepKind
        FillRule
        LineCap
        LineJoin

    @guarantee ThirtyCommandsAreReadOnceAndDrawnThreeTimes
        -- The dialect is read here, in the domain, into paint instructions.
        -- A desktop window, a browser and a phone each execute those and
        -- decide nothing, so a command is understood once and drawn as many
        -- times as there are renderers.
        --
        -- Rebol's C fused the two in host-draw.c, and the cost of that is
        -- visible in the source we vendor: there is a win32 host-draw.c and no
        -- posix one, so a stock R3 on macOS or Linux draws nothing at all.

    @guarantee WhatIsNotPaintedYetIsNamedRatherThanHidden
        -- Gradients, dash patterns, images, text, arrows and Gouraud shading
        -- are not painted. Each is left out because the two renderers would
        -- not agree about it, not because it was forgotten, and a command
        -- nobody serves is skipped so that the rest of a block still draws.
        --
        -- The gap is the same gap everywhere, because it is decided in one
        -- place. A renderer cannot quietly support something the others do
        -- not.
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Open questions
------------------------------------------------------------

open question "Should a command this build does not paint be skipped or refused? Skipped is what this says, on the grounds that a picture missing one shape is more use than no picture. But the binary dialect made the opposite call for a reason that half applies here: a caller who writes `grad-pen` and gets a flat fill has been told nothing, and may ship it. Would a way to ask -- draw/strict, or a warning collected somewhere a script can read -- be better than either?"

open question "A stroked curve will not compare pixel for pixel between Java2D and a browser canvas. Line joins, miter limits and how a cap is drawn on a curve all differ slightly, and no shared input fixes it because the difference is in the rasteriser. The flat-rectangle comparison in browserCheck needs a stated tolerance for these, or the curve cases need to assert the path rather than the pixels. Which?"

open question "The C's defaults read the gob's size, so `box` alone fills the gob and `circle` alone is the biggest circle that fits. That is lovely for writing by hand and it means a draw block means different things on gobs of different sizes -- which is either the point or a trap, depending on whether somebody expected a block to be portable between gobs. Nothing in the C or the documentation says which."

open question "SPLINE takes a segmentation count and draws a curve through its points, and nothing anywhere says which interpolation. AGG has a specific one; a browser has none built in. Implementing it means picking a spline and being wrong somewhere. Is it worth having at all, or is CURVE enough?"
